From 50af60bb65cbe71f08cbdda9976385f8a3254835 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: claude <joe@joemoser.com>
Date: Sun, 16 Aug 2026 16:24:57 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(daemon): stop re-authing a session Apple already declared
 dead
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

When Apple returns 421 ("Authentication required for Account"), pyicloud raises
PyiCloudFailedLoginException("Invalid authentication token.") and the daemon
alerts + exits(1). Under KeepAlive/ThrottleInterval=300 launchd restarts it five
minutes later, and startup calls /accountLogin with the same dead token again —
an unbounded loop (~288 failed auth attempts/day), visible in afm-daemon.log as
10:15 -> 10:54 repeating identically.

Only util/auth.sh can mint a new session, and repeated auth attempts are exactly
what makes Apple rate-limit SRP and withhold trust tokens, so the restart loop was
sabotaging the auth.sh run that fixes it.

afm_session_hold.SessionHold records the rejection on disk. While the hold is
active the daemon short-circuits at startup without contacting Apple, re-probing
on an escalating 15m -> 30m -> 60m schedule (~24 attempts/day instead of ~288).
The hold is keyed to a fingerprint of ~/.pyicloud, so auth.sh installing a new
session clears it automatically on the next restart — recovery is unchanged and
still hands-off. Every corrupt/unreadable state path fails open, so the hold can
never keep a healthy session offline.

Transient failures (5xx/429/network) are untouched and keep the 5-minute cadence;
the hold applies only to sessions Apple has actually rejected.

Tests: 34 new cases, no dependencies (pyicloud/BigQuery stubbed).
test_daemon_hold.test_restart_after_rejection_does_not_contact_apple is the
reproducer — it fails without this change (12 auth calls/hour, 1 with it).
