 ## Behavior notes
 
 - **Session death** (needs `auth.sh`): the daemon sends one Pushcut alert
-  (deduped 30 min) and exits; launchd restarts it every `ThrottleInterval` (300s).
-  After you run `auth.sh`, the next restart picks up the fresh session — no manual
-  daemon restart needed.
+  (deduped 30 min), records a **dead-session hold**, and exits; launchd restarts it
+  every `ThrottleInterval` (300s). Each restart while the hold is active
+  short-circuits **without contacting Apple** — it logs `session is on the
+  dead-session hold` and exits. Apple is re-probed on an escalating schedule
+  (15 min → 30 min → 60 min, capped), so a dead session costs ~24 auth attempts a
+  day instead of ~288. After you run `auth.sh`, the next restart picks up the fresh
+  session — no manual daemon restart needed.
+
+  *Why the hold exists:* only `auth.sh` can revive a dead session, and repeatedly
+  re-calling `/accountLogin` with a token Apple has already rejected is precisely
+  what triggers SRP rate-limiting and withheld trust tokens — i.e. the restart loop
+  was sabotaging the `auth.sh` run that fixes it. State lives in
+  `~/.pyicloud-daemon-session-dead` and is keyed to a fingerprint of `~/.pyicloud`,
+  so installing a new session clears it automatically. **Never delete it by hand as
+  a recovery step** — run `auth.sh`. (Deleting it is harmless but pointless: it only
+  buys one immediate retry against a session that is still dead.)
 - **Transient errors** (Apple 5xx/429, network, an un-self-healed 450): logged,
   retried after 60s, session kept in memory. No exit.
 - **Data-plane errors** (BigQuery/pandas/alerts): logged + alerted, but the daemon
